{"case_id": "MB_18", "timestep": 5, "case_summary": "A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain. The pain has been dull and progressive, but became suddenly worse while she was exercising. Her medical history is notable for depression, anxiety, and gonococcal urethritis that was appropriately treated. She is sexually active and does not use condoms. She admits to drinking at least 5 standard alcoholic drinks a day. The patient also recently lost a large amount of weight for a fitness show she planned on entering. The patient's current medications include oral contraceptive pills, fluoxetine, alprazolam, ibuprofen, acetaminophen, and folate. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 80/40 mmHg, pulse is 110/minute, and respirations are 15/minute with an oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. On physical exam, you note an athletic young woman with burly shoulders, a thick neck, and acne on her forehead and back. On abdominal exam you note diffuse tenderness with 10/10 pain upon palpation of the right upper quadrant. Intravenous fluids are started and labs are sent. A urinary ß-hCG has been ordered. Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst\nB. Inflammation of the pancreas\nC. Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein\nD. Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones\nE. Vascular ectasia within the liver\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Hemodynamic Instability with Severe Right Upper Quadrant Pain and Constitutional Alarm Signs", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.5, "supporting_facts": ["Severe RUQ pain with diffuse tenderness", "Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm)", "Temperature 37.5°C (constitutional alarm sign)", "History of gonococcal urethritis and high-risk sexual behavior", "Recent significant weight loss and alcohol use disorder"], "excluded_candidates": ["Chronic hepatic dysfunction with acute decompensation: less likely due to sudden onset and hemodynamic instability", "Exertional rhabdomyolysis with systemic complications: unlikely as primary explanation without CK elevation", "Ovarian pathology (e.g., torsion, abscess): could explain RUQ pain but doesn't account for hemodynamic collapse"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension (BP 80/40 mmHg)", "Tachycardia (pulse 110/minute)", "Temperature 37.5°C"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic / Pelvic Source of Acute Hemodynamic Instability and RUQ Pain", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.19434704404486663, "posterior": 0.24813875369721353, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The subacute presentation with constitutional symptoms and hemodynamic instability is consistent with gynecologic sepsis (e.g., ruptured abscess), moderately s…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Source of Acute Hemodynamic Instability and RUQ Pain", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.1453808888879355, "posterior": 0.18561966176196262, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Chronic alcohol use is a known risk factor for Budd-Chiari syndrome, but the absence of jaundice/ascites in the physical exam creates uncertainty, resulting in…"], "evidence_against": ["The absence of classical Budd-Chiari signs (jaundice, hepatomegaly) weakens this diagnosis despite alcohol risk factors."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Biliary Obstruction with Secondary Hemodynamic Compromise", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.057, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of biliary risk factors substantially reduces the probability of biliary obstruction as the primary cause."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Pancreatic Source of Acute Hemodynamic Instability and RUQ Pain", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.057, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of both typical pain pattern and pending enzymatic confirmation strongly argues against pancreatitis as the primary cause."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Hepatic Vascular Ectasia with Hemodynamic Instability", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.018, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["These physical signs are more consistent with Cushing's syndrome than with hepatic vascular ectasia, but Cushing's is not currently a considered branch."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum and Hemodynamic Instability", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.18172611110991935, "posterior": 0.23202457720245326, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The ordering of β-hCG is a critical diagnostic step for ectopic pregnancy, though results are pending. A positive result would strongly support this diagnosis."], "evidence_against": ["The absence of a missed period or vaginal bleeding slightly reduces the probability of ectopic pregnancy, though β-hCG results remain pending."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.97, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Ruptured Ovarian Abscess with Septic Shock", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.016, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Cushing’s features are more suggestive of hormonal pathology than sepsis, further weakening the ruptured ovarian abscess hypothesis."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Septic Abortion with Hemodynamic Instability", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.12115074073994625, "posterior": 0.1546830514683022, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The subacute presentation with constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) and hypotension is more typical of Budd-Chiari syndrome than acute gynecologic seps…"], "evidence_against": ["Classic Budd-Chiari signs (jaundice, ascites) are absent, reducing confidence in this diagnosis despite alcohol use as a risk factor."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Peliosis Hepatis with Hemorrhagic Complications", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.031, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Cushing’s features are more likely to explain the physical findings than peliosis hepatis, which lacks specific clinical associations in this context."]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B2.1", "B1.1", "B2.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of a missed period or vaginal bleeding in the history weaken the likelihood of ectopic pregnancy as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "The absence of a missed period or vaginal bleeding slightly reduces the probability of ectopic pregnancy, though β-hCG results remain pending."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of Cushing's syndrome features (burly shoulders, acne) argue against ruptured ovarian abscess despite sepsis risk factors?", "summary": "Cushing’s features are more suggestive of hormonal pathology than sepsis, further weakening the ruptured ovarian abscess hypothesis."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of jaundice, ascites, or hepatomegaly in the physical exam create a significant safety gap for Budd-Chiari syndrome despite chronic alcohol use?", "summary": "Classic Budd-Chiari signs (jaundice, ascites) are absent, reducing confidence in this diagnosis despite alcohol use as a risk factor."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the Cushing's syndrome features (burly shoulders, acne) more consistent with Cushing's than with peliosis hepatis as a cause of RUQ pain and hypotension?", "summary": "Cushing’s features are more likely to explain the physical findings than peliosis hepatis, which lacks specific clinical associations in this context."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of Cushing’s syndrome features (burly shoulders, acne) and absence of sepsis indicators (e.g., leukocytosis) create a stronger argument against gynecologic/pelvic pathology as the p…", "summary": "The presence of Cushing's syndrome features (burly shoulders, acne) and absence of sepsis indicators (e.g., leukocytosis) moderately weaken the gynecologic/pelvic pathology hypothesis as the primary cause of hemodynamic instability and RUQ pain. This shifts suspicion toward hormonal or other non-in…"}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset with constitutional symptoms and hemodynamic instability argue for gynecologic sepsis (e.g., ruptured abscess) as the primary cause rather than hepatic or biliary pathology?", "summary": "The subacute onset with constitutional symptoms and hemodynamic instability provides moderate support for gynecologic/pelvic pathology (B1) as a potential cause, though the absence of specific sepsis indicators and presence of Cushing’s features (burly shoulders, acne) reduce the strength of this a…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.365], ["B1.1", 0.342], ["B2", 0.137]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.194], ["B1.1", 0.182], ["B2", 0.145]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.248], ["B1.1", 0.232], ["B2", 0.186]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) with end-organ signs (right upper quadrant pain). Constitutional alarm signs (fever, weight loss) and sepsis risk factors (recent gonococcal urethritis, unprotected sex, alcohol-induced liver risk) indicate major haemodynamic instability requiring stabilization.", "required_actions": ["Aggressive IV fluid resuscitation", "Monitor response to fluids", "Consider vasopressors if hypotension persists", "Check lactate and CBC for sepsis", "Urgent imaging (e.g., ultrasound) for Budd-Chiari syndrome"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B1.1: Ectopic Pregnancy) posterior 0.232 < 0.75. Dangerous alternatives (B1.1 danger 0.9, B2.1 danger 0.85) remain unresolved with posterior ≥0.10. β-hCG results pending before actionable diagnosis can be confirmed. Emergency stabilization ongoing but requires further diagnostic refinement."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.55, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.23202457720245326, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'pain', 'acne', 'hypotension', 'Biliary cirrhosis', 'obstruction', 'pelvic inflammatory disease', 'Sepsis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.476}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum and Hemodynamic Instability", "posterior": 0.232, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The absence of a missed period or vaginal bleeding slightly reduces the probability of ectopic pregnancy, though β-hCG …"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.1", "B3", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=40%, source=primekg]\n\nBudd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis vs Peliosis Hepatis with Hemorrhagic Complications:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nRelated to Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis: hepatic vascular disease\n\nRelated to Peliosis Hepatis with Hemorrhagic Complications: hepatic vascular disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → pain → Peliosis Hepatis with Hemorrhagic Complications\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → shoulder pain → Peliosis Hepatis with Hemorrhagic Complications\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → abdominal pain → Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Hepatic Vein Thrombosis"}
